<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; Justice" cic="CCC 1877-1948" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in groups. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Recalling the essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What is the classic definition of justice that the Catechism incorporates? Why does it matter that it is a «constant and firm will»?</li>
<li>Name and explain the three types of justice. Give a concrete example of each from your daily life.</li>
<li>What does the «universal destination of goods» mean? Why is private property not an absolute right?</li>
<li>Name the four fundamental principles of the Church's social doctrine and briefly explain each one.</li>
<li>What does the Catechism say about restitution? Why doesn't forgiveness cancel it?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For reflection and writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question sincerely. There are no «correct» answers to these personal questions.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. What things do you owe other people that you have not yet paid back or returned?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Make a list of 3 concrete things. Not just money: time, attention, recognition, apologies, gratitude. Be specific with names and situations.</p>
<p class="question-block__p" style="margin-top:8px">___________ ___________ ___________</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. If Jesus told you today, as he told Zacchaeus: «Come down, for I am staying at your house today,» what would change in your economic life? What expense would embarrass you? What debt would you repair?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Be brutally honest. The answer is between you and God.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. Of the sins against the seventh commandment (theft, fraud, usury, unjust wages, corruption, tax evasion, harm to creation), which one seems most normalized in your culture? Have you participated in that normalization?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">For example: «everyone cheats on taxes,» «you can't get ahead without bribes,» «it's just business.» Think about those phrases people say without thinking.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. Imagine you are the owner of a company with 200 employees. What would your wage policy be? How much would you earn compared to the lowest-paid employee? Why?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">The gap between the highest and lowest salary at a company says a lot about its idea of justice.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. Have you forgiven a grave injustice without giving up on justice being done? How do you do that? What does Our Lady of Guadalupe teach us about how God restores justice without vengeance?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Because justice without mercy is vengeance. But mercy without justice is complicity.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical activities</h3>

<h4>A. Zacchaeus's balance sheet</h4>
<p>Make your own «justice balance sheet» Zacchaeus-style. In one column, write what you have received without deserving it (gifts, opportunities, privileges). In another, write what you have taken without right or not paid. In a third, write what you commit to giving back, to whom, and by when. Don't file it away: act this week.</p>

